[{:db/id "q-94731/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f70a9f8b-7811-5d36-894b-0818ed763318" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "52c7f4f6-6077-546f-a7fd-db8a4f24420b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "76f3fde2-0b97-541a-b257-2c4422376b47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2638fec9-d27a-5a77-b823-343d8ea0c08d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "41171f9a-bd23-5bf9-b4fb-eb0bb4181f48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5544bb8-4bac-525c-986f-d947aa317309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94731/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94731/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94731/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-94731/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94731/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94731/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94731" :question/id #uuid "f1e99ebc-7a14-5960-833b-4a49e1eca26c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94731" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94731/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "12e44b57-00b0-5e17-bb4d-57b17349f456" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b2f3c102-d706-5e07-82fc-f7056f2f9d36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "30fe2f8c-2f6e-55e8-a9ca-0097ce0487c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "10289142-329b-5cb8-b39a-7a56c9ac66fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c4b4209b-a63d-5686-b7e3-615bda8f9307" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c6d3913-98b1-5983-9c30-56a99dba7e95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139436/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-139436/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-139436/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-139436/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-139436/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-139436/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139436" :question/id #uuid "a8ff653f-0db2-5e4b-99bc-705651d85f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-139436" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139436/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139436"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "0f813a63-ed9b-556c-90f4-ee76f89ffaeb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "05a1f8cc-a779-54d8-8947-0cf2d637e366" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2381036d-ee34-5f27-9d82-953f70ba7173" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "46148325-6813-531c-a8ea-47eac22bde8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2bd169a7-79c1-5b07-9f3a-b3319ece1659" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "96080315-dd72-5098-b4a0-7029a90b9712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94744/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-94744/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-94744/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94744/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94744/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94744/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94744" :question/id #uuid "adc08558-14b4-5986-ac1e-110d6dbcb8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94744" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94744/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94462"] :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ef6573d8-cbc2-559c-98e3-2010f618983d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ac3715d0-9134-52d8-8a54-8abfbe3b9c98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "af91df7e-460b-5c9c-ac85-2dcda8fb703a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "2b42d044-3e6b-5f93-9b21-885d9eec3750" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "a04fb800-ae92-5204-954d-621006726402" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "607cafa2-10fb-5b2d-8514-3267070a098d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94734/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94734/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94734/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94734/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94734/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94734/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94734" :question/id #uuid "8eb37d11-7d5f-52ca-ac94-2177d57f1e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-94734" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94734/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "3583e224-614f-5358-91d8-d15e1ddfd354" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "5bafb3d2-a6f6-5621-b970-da9c04468c87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ae69af1c-e935-556d-94f6-3408d2cc4e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "f389cd8c-f5a1-5299-b53e-136523cb6b14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d911e49f-3a68-59e5-91cb-f919839b3fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5538a150-5498-5bc7-8852-22eaef7e1c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139457/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-139457/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-139457/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-139457/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-139457/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-139457/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139457" :question/id #uuid "2f224bef-0344-53d7-b951-4a61b68ffa98" :question/math-academy-id "q-139457" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- $A$ is negative semi-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139457/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139457"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94465"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4e23e3c4-d850-5d13-a01d-ba2d0cca3553" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8c8e7925-1582-565d-a9ef-9f258b5eb138" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "bc53ae43-7a32-531c-8a19-e18b2b91ba6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "92cd0cae-ca4f-57e3-9c48-460919c7d247" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "83df3c94-37fb-55c0-a932-fdc0ba4126f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e99ae93d-056a-5953-a0af-3a20582b2cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139458/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-139458/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-139458/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-139458/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-139458/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-139458/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139458" :question/id #uuid "75e77ccb-4a65-5d95-ac73-d5342f2664e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139458" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139458/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "cf5013a5-5693-5e28-a9c8-8475d8172182" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "12f83590-6ee5-58ca-b0db-f7a45a178f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "f71b612d-f31e-53a7-91bf-b8b178db19b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b41164b6-7b8c-538c-a7ff-2bc9ebcc0852" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5bc18864-a972-5022-9f2f-d42c26fa539f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "61b19d22-b7ee-502e-bcf6-3d819a75ba12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139460/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-139460/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-139460/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-139460/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-139460/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-139460/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139460" :question/id #uuid "c2a5cae0-eaad-5232-a7d3-51a520ed57c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139460" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "0310574b-4390-5ee9-97d7-3ef3e67d2c01" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d4f593b0-fd3c-560e-8899-1ec277b63665" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6cd054b5-ecc2-57a8-9fdd-8f3ff6af10ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "aedad796-c4e4-5813-a9ca-8219432fdfee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9f742d72-24da-5d08-a8fb-de952385988f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d899bf-6bf7-53fd-8054-85ec15073d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94795/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-94795/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-94795/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94795/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94795/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94795/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94795" :question/id #uuid "a9d123b6-e467-5363-8d7e-2f42b2dd2007" :question/math-academy-id "q-94795" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94795/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94795"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d098fb59-d221-5232-aaa1-ba1f94608da1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "61bf7c48-36de-5704-8efd-ef8217bf1232" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "0aea0f56-ef0c-5964-bc85-b0dae033e076" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "ca320700-0b10-53ca-aab2-7a521ec52a76" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "9741c286-dbd8-58af-a11e-bead141d1ced" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "064fa492-4f62-596b-bba0-96647de03e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94806/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94806/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94806/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94806/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94806/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94806/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94806" :question/id #uuid "65811339-aac9-5ec0-920b-aa92354d43ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-94806" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94806/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94806"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "d9d30edd-82ca-54a4-9dfe-ea7842e6d222" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9f49ec7a-ac37-5f00-91dd-58e6f6b8e770" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7e800f3e-becd-5e15-9adb-f907547a3048" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b3098e45-a99c-5a5a-ad56-8b7a0087eaa0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "653c3d48-c980-5b54-b74d-dfe72edb199b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f013a1-dc6b-5efb-9e32-3f3ac338057d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94801/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-94801/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94801/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94801/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-94801/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94801/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94801" :question/id #uuid "c39a7f0f-4c12-51dc-813b-487dd65653a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94801" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94801/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94801"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "49882f2c-e639-5500-895b-c44ed288e2a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "792690f3-8458-543e-b05c-3d39a6efae0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "46558a42-4bb9-5465-9939-2070630e1f36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "2877271a-f851-54d9-abb3-45225ca257cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7718b83a-b5ca-5c58-b430-9c4d5a049d9d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c6a1b9-63dc-55cf-a2b3-8cb22656e587" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94797/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-94797/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94797/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-94797/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94797/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94797/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94797" :question/id #uuid "cd98f9d6-181c-53f6-90aa-fa78d6837c3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94797" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94797/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94797"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "019bc127-f2b2-5d76-b574-320d405890d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "23aa71c1-10f2-536a-9656-e8e0f4bd2c1b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fd3c3a8c-4613-5148-9e30-ec1000e66629" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "de3aa0c3-281c-572a-81e1-a92bfacc2677" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bc7240a4-b2b6-5412-9b25-272d2feba451" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfd2b46e-3d30-5d11-9f46-beb860994b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139462/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-139462/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-139462/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-139462/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-139462/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-139462/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139462" :question/id #uuid "061e6504-cfaf-51e0-88ae-f11dcba1d1dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-139462" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139462/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139462"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94467"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8262"] :question/problem "Consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/cc0456884aa670c8c7f86387574a187f151f4ccc4e4275671425af1aa61812e8.png) By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/acf8b72ee7b927e5e82570c655a671a62b595a29c8191a64ad22ac98af8cf7a4.png)\n\n- $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/29545f79f3b34ca5395f98fa8c8c81e289482e866c3ad8c576aeb7b9fe8b8749.png)\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={2}^{2}-3\\cdot {7}^{2}+5\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =4-147+70 \\\\  & =-73<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =110>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={7}^{2}+8\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =49+112 \\\\  & =161>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8263"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d16c12fa9086e956b3b0516d42b40aaa121e3b40bd25a72c3d05d436ba410440.png) Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8264"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d81ce57017adec7daf2556322259f32ce4261e646dc8f1a48632beb085d9a9cb.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-5)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
